ss","H8085":"hear","H6963":"voice","H4735":"livestock","H5775":"bird","H8064":"heaven","H5704":"till","H929":"animal","H5074":"to wander","H3389":"Jerusalem","H1530":"heap","H4583":"habitation","H8577":"jackal","H5892":"excitement","H3063":"Judah","H8077":"devastation","H2450":"wise","H995":"to understand","H2063":"this","H5046":"to tell","H4100":"what?","H6":"to perish","H8451":"instruction","H310":"after","H8307":"stubbornness","H3820":"heart","H1168":"Baal","H1":"father","H430":"God","H3478":"Israel","H398":"to eat","H3939":"wormwood","H8248":"watering","H7219":"poison","H6327":"to scatter","H1992":"they(masc.)","H7971":"depart","H2719":"sword","H3615":"finish","H7121":"call to","H6969":"to chant","H935":"come","H4116":"to hasten","H3381":"to go down","H6079":"eyelid","H5140":"to flow","H6726":"Zion","H7703":"to ruin","H954":"be ashamed","H3966":"much","H7993":"to throw","H4908":"tabernacle","H802":"woman","H1697":"word","H3947":"take","H241":"ear","H7468":"neighbor","H5927":"rise","H4194":"death","H2474":"window","H759":"palace","H3772":"cut","H5768":"infant","H2351":"outside","H970":"youth","H7339":"street/plaza","H5307":"fall","H5038":"carcass","H120":"man","H1828":"dung","H7704":"Sirion","H5995":"sheaf","H7114":"be short","H369":"nothing","H622":"to gather","H1984":"to shine","H2451":"wisdom","H1368":"mighty man","H1369":"might","H6223":"rich","H6239":"riches","H7919":"be prudent","H589":"I","H2617":"kindness","H4941":"judgement","H6666":"righteousness","H2654":"to delight in","H2009":"behold","H3117":"day","H4135":"to circumcise","H6190":"(Gilgal)-haaraloth","H4714":"Egypt","H123":"Edom","H1121":"child","H5983":"Ammon","H4124":"Moab","H7112":"to cut","H6285":"side","H6189":"uncircumcised","H1004":"place"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","To. Hebrew is more expressive. (Calmet) — \"Who will give my head waters, (Haydock) or change it into water, and my eyes into a fountain.\" (Calmet) — Protestants \"Oh that my head were waters.\" (Haydock) — The miseries of my people are so great, (Calmet) a few tears would not suffice to bewail them. (Worthington)"],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Men. Why cannot I retire (Menochius) from this scene of misery, or afford some consolation to the captives? Septuagint \"Who will give me the last station in the desert?\" (Haydock)"],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Lies. They are bent on deceit (Calmet) and murder, (Haydock) v. 8. Ps. lxiii. 4. and cxix. 2."],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Deceitfully. A man must guard against his relations and domestics. Mic. vii. 5. Mat. viii. 35. (Calmet)"],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Iniquity. It is become natural to them. (St. Jerome) — They cannot lay the blame on the violence of passion. They sin on purpose. (Calmet) — Septuagint \"they have not left off, (6.) in order to be converted. Usury upon usury; fraud upon fraud; they,\" &c. (Haydock)"],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Try them in the crucible of war. C. vi. 27. (Calmet)"],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Owner. Hebrew mikne, \"cattle,\" or (Haydock) \"substance.\" (St. Jerome) — Departed. Beasts and birds will not continue long after men cease to cultivate the country. Theod. C. iv. 25. and xii. 4. Soph. i. 3."],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Sand. The stones were burnt to dust. 2 Esd. iv. 2. (Calmet)"],["haydock",15,null,"haydock","Gall. Hebrew rosh, \"poison,\" &c. Deut. xxix. 18. (Haydock)"],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Consumed. C. xliv. 27. No country shall afford them protection. (Calmet) — The richer sort were made captives, and most of the people destroyed, but not all. C. iv. 25. (Worthington)"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Wise, in composing or singing the Nænia, or mournful songs, recording the praises of the deceased. (Calmet) — \"This custom still subsists in Judea: women go about with dishevelled hair and naked breasts, with mournful tunes, exciting all to tears.\" (St. Jerome) — Music was also used. Mat. ix. 23. Thus feigned tears, at least, would supply the want of real ones."],["haydock",21,null,"haydock","For. This was the song. (Calmet) — Streets. Death spares none. The least suspecting fall. (Haydock) — It enters by the windows, if the doors be shut. Joel ii. 8."],["haydock",22,null,"haydock","Speak. Our editions of the Septuagint omit this word, though S. Jerom found it in his copies, (Calmet) and in Theodotion, expressed by \"death;\" while others rendered it, \"speak,\" as the same letters dbr, may have both meanings, according as people may please to pronounce them. (St. Jerome) — Grabe supplies, \"by death. (22.) These things, says the Lord.\" (Haydock)"],["haydock",24,null,"haydock","Me. Virtue will save, when riches, &c. will prove useless. (Menochius) — Phocilides, a pagan, said, (Calmet) \"Boast not of wisdom, strength, or riches great. One God is wise, and potent too, and rich.\" (Haydock)"],["haydock",26,null,"haydock","Egypt. the uncircumcised shall be punished as well as Juda, if they transgress. Only the Jewish nation properly observed the right of circumcision generally, or at first. Others imitated them, but with various ceremonies. C. Diss. Ezec. xxxi. 18. Judith xiv. 6. — Hyrcan obliged the Idumeans to receive circumcision. Jos. Ant. xiii. 17. — Round. The Arabs, &c. Lev. xix. 27. (Calmet) — Heart. All then became guilty. Rom. ii. 25. (Menochius) — Neither these nations nor Juda was circumcised in heart, and of course were hypocrites. (Worthington)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]},"fa":[[1,null,0,"Conferences of John Cassian - Part I","9. The First Conference of Abbot Isaac",0,435,"… There is too another kind of tears, which are caused not by knowledge of one's self but by the hardness and sins of others; whereby Samuel is described as having wept for Saul, and both the Lord in the gospel and Jeremiah in former days for the city of Jerusalem, the latter thus saying: \"Oh, that my head were water and mine eyes a fountain of tears! And I will weep day and night for the slain of the daughter of my people. Or also such as were those tears of which we hear in the hundred and first Psalm: \"For I have eaten ashes for my bread, and mingled my cup with weeping. …"],[5,null,0,"Conferences of John Cassian - Part III","23. The Third Conference of Abbot Theonas",0,435,"… as he says: \"They have laboured to do wickedly.\""],[17,null,1,"Homilies on John","Homily 64",1,407,"… We profit nothing by our prayers, nothing by our advice and exhortation, it remaineth therefore that we weep. Thus did Christ; after having many times exhorted those in Jerusalem, when they profited nothing, He wept at their hardness. This also do the Prophets, and this let us do now. Henceforth is the season for mourning and tears and wailing; it is seasonable for us also to say now, \"Call for the mourning women, and send for the cunning women, that they may cry aloud\" (Jer. ix. 17); perhaps thus we shall be able to cast out the malady of those who build splendid houses, of those who surround themselves with lands gotten by rapine."],[23,24,2,"First Epistle to the Corinthians","Clement's First Letter to the Corinthians, Chapter 13",2,99,"Let us therefore, brethren, be of humble mind, laying aside all haughtiness, and pride, and foolishness, and angry feelings; and let us act according to that which is written (for the Holy Spirit saith, \"Let not the wise man glory in his wisdom, neither let the mighty man glory in his might, neither let the rich man glory in his riches; but let him that glorieth glory in the Lord, in diligently seeking Him, and doing judgment and righteousness\"), being especially mindful of the words of the Lord Jesus which He spake, teaching us meekness and long-suffering. …"],[23,24,3,"Treatise 12. Three Books of Testimonies Against the Jews","Treatise XII. Three Books of Testimonies Against the Jews 3:10",3,258,"We must trust in God only, and in him we must glory. In Jeremiah: \"Let not the wise man glory in his wisdom, neither let the strong man glory in his strength, nor let the rich man glory in his riches. But let him that glories glory in this, that he understands and knows that I am the Lord, who does mercy, and judgment, and righteousness upon the earth, because in them is my pleasure, says the Lord.\""],[24,null,4,"On the Merits and Forgiveness of Sins, and on the Baptism of Infants","Book 2",4,430,"Forasmuch then as our turning away from God is our own act, and this is evil will; but our turning to God is not possible, except He rouses and helps us, and this is good will,—what have we that we have not received? But if we received, why do we glory as if we had not received? Therefore, as \"he that glorieth must glory in the Lord,\" it comes from His mercy, not their merit, that God wills to impart this to some, but from His truth that He wills not to impart it to others. For to sinners punishment is justly due."],[24,null,4,"Treatise on Rebuke and Grace","ON REBUKE AND GRACE 28",4,430,"For as many of this race as are delivered by God's grace are certainly delivered from the condemnation in which they are already held bound. Whence, even if none should be delivered, no one could justly blame the judgment of God. That, therefore, in comparison of those that perish few, but in their absolute number many, are delivered, is effected by grace, is effected freely: thanks must be given, because it is effected, so that no one may be lifted up as of his own deservings, but that every mouth may be stopped, and he that glorieth may glory in the Lord."]],"fau":[["cassian","John Cassian",0,"father",null,0,435],["chrysostom","John Chrysostom",1,"father",null,1,407],["clement-of-rome","Clement of Rome",0,"father",null,0],["cyprian","Cyprian of Carthage",0,"father",null,0,258],["augustine","Augustine of Hippo",1,"father",null,1,430]],"ftr":[["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 11 (1894)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf211"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 1, vol. 14 (1889)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf114"],["Ante-Nicene Fathers, vol. 1 (1885)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/anf01"],["Ante-Nicene Fathers, vol. 5 (1886)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/anf05"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 1, vol. 5 (1887)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf105"]]}